Task 1: Install Grafana on a Linux Server
Subtask 1.1: Update System and Install Prerequisites
First, ensure your system is up to date and install necessary dependencies.

# Update package repositories
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install required packages
sudo apt install -y software-properties-common apt-transport-https wget curl gnupg2
Subtask 1.2: Add Grafana Repository
Add the official Grafana repository to your system's package sources.

# Add Grafana GPG key
wget -q -O - https://packages.grafana.com/gpg.key | sudo apt-key add -

# Add Grafana repository
echo "deb https://packages.grafana.com/oss/deb stable main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/grafana.list

# Update package list with new repository
sudo apt update
Subtask 1.3: Install Grafana Server
Install the Grafana server package and verify the installation.

# Install Grafana
sudo apt install -y grafana

# Verify installation
grafana-server --version

# Check if Grafana service is available
systemctl status grafana-server
Subtask 1.4: Start and Enable Grafana Service
Configure Grafana to start automatically and begin the service.

# Enable Grafana to start on boot
sudo systemctl enable grafana-server

# Start Grafana service
sudo systemctl start grafana-server

# Verify service is running
sudo systemctl status grafana-server

# Check if Grafana is listening on port 3000
sudo netstat -tlnp | grep 3000
Task 2: Configure Grafana for First Use
Subtask 2.1: Access Grafana Web Interface
Open your web browser and navigate to the Grafana interface.

# Check your machine's IP address
ip addr show | grep inet

# Note: Grafana runs on port 3000 by default
# Access via: http://YOUR_IP_ADDRESS:3000
Default Login Credentials:

Username: admin
Password: admin
Subtask 2.2: Change Default Admin Password
For security purposes, change the default admin password immediately.

Log in with default credentials (admin/admin)
You will be prompted to change the password
Set a strong password and remember it
Click Submit to save the new password
Subtask 2.3: Configure Basic Grafana Settings
Modify the main configuration file to customize basic settings.

# Backup original configuration
sudo cp /etc/grafana/grafana.ini /etc/grafana/grafana.ini.backup

# Edit configuration file
sudo nano /etc/grafana/grafana.ini
Key Configuration Changes:

# Under [server] section
http_port = 3000
domain = localhost
root_url = http://localhost:3000

# Under [security] section
admin_user = admin
admin_password = your_secure_password

# Under [users] section
allow_sign_up = false
allow_org_create = false

# Under [auth.anonymous] section
enabled = false
Subtask 2.4: Restart Grafana Service
Apply configuration changes by restarting the service.

# Restart Grafana to apply changes
sudo systemctl restart grafana-server

# Verify service is running properly
sudo systemctl status grafana-server

# Check logs for any errors
sudo journalctl -u grafana-server -f
Task 3: Connect to a Local Data Source
Subtask 3.1: Install Prometheus as Data Source
Install Prometheus to serve as a local data source for Grafana.

# Create prometheus user
sudo useradd --no-create-home --shell /bin/false prometheus

# Create directories
sudo mkdir /etc/prometheus
sudo mkdir /var/lib/prometheus

# Set ownership
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /var/lib/prometheus

# Download Prometheus
cd /tmp
wget https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/releases/download/v2.40.0/prometheus-2.40.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Extract and install
tar xvf prometheus-2.40.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz
cd prometheus-2.40.0.linux-amd64

# Copy binaries
sudo cp prometheus /usr/local/bin/
sudo cp promtool /usr/local/bin/

# Set ownership
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /usr/local/bin/prometheus
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /usr/local/bin/promtool

# Copy configuration files
sudo cp -r consoles /etc/prometheus
sudo cp -r console_libraries /etc/prometheus
sudo chown -R prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/consoles
sudo chown -R prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/console_libraries
Subtask 3.2: Configure Prometheus
Create a basic Prometheus configuration file.

# Create Prometheus configuration
sudo nano /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Prometheus Configuration:

global:
  scrape_interval: 15s

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: 'prometheus'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9090']
  
  - job_name: 'node'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9100']
# Set ownership
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Subtask 3.3: Create Prometheus Service
Create a systemd service file for Prometheus.

# Create service file
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/prometheus.service
Service Configuration:

[Unit]
Description=Prometheus
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=prometheus
Group=prometheus
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/prometheus \
    --config.file /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml \
    --storage.tsdb.path /var/lib/prometheus/ \
    --web.console.templates=/etc/prometheus/consoles \
    --web.console.libraries=/etc/prometheus/console_libraries

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
Subtask 3.4: Start Prometheus Service
Enable and start the Prometheus service.

# Reload systemd
sudo systemctl daemon-reload

# Enable and start Prometheus
sudo systemctl enable prometheus
sudo systemctl start prometheus

# Check status
sudo systemctl status prometheus

# Verify Prometheus is running
curl http://localhost:9090/metrics
Subtask 3.5: Install Node Exporter
Install Node Exporter to provide system metrics.

# Create node_exporter user
sudo useradd --no-create-home --shell /bin/false node_exporter

# Download Node Exporter
cd /tmp
wget https://github.com/prometheus/node_exporter/releases/download/v1.5.0/node_exporter-1.5.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Extract and install
tar xvf node_exporter-1.5.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz
sudo cp node_exporter-1.5.0.linux-amd64/node_exporter /usr/local/bin/

# Set ownership
sudo chown node_exporter:node_exporter /usr/local/bin/node_exporter
Subtask 3.6: Create Node Exporter Service
# Create service file
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/node_exporter.service
Node Exporter Service Configuration:

[Unit]
Description=Node Exporter
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=node_exporter
Group=node_exporter
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/node_exporter

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
# Start Node Exporter
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable node_exporter
sudo systemctl start node_exporter

# Verify service
sudo systemctl status node_exporter
curl http://localhost:9100/metrics
Subtask 3.7: Add Prometheus Data Source to Grafana
Open Grafana web interface (http://localhost:3000)
Log in with your admin credentials
Click on Configuration (gear icon) in the left sidebar
Select Data Sources
Click Add data source
Select Prometheus
Configure the data source:
Name: Prometheus
URL: http://localhost:9090
Access: Server (default)
Click Save & Test
Subtask 3.8: Verify Data Source Connection
Test the connection and explore available metrics.

# Verify Prometheus targets
curl http://localhost:9090/api/v1/targets

# Check available metrics
curl http://localhost:9090/api/v1/label/__name__/values
In Grafana:

Go to Explore in the left sidebar
Select Prometheus as the data source
Try querying metrics like:
up
node_cpu_seconds_total
node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes
Subtask 3.9: Create a Simple Dashboard
Create your first dashboard with basic system metrics.

Click + (Create) in the left sidebar
Select Dashboard
Click Add new panel
Configure the panel:
Query: rate(node_cpu_seconds_total[5m])
Panel Title: CPU Usage Rate
